speechtext:
It is a very popular way of dealing with this question to say: Let us give the people rural mail deliveries, without considering who is going to pay for it. The question is, can we afford under the present condition of our finances to expand this luxury much more than we have done? My hon. friend probably has not studied the public accounts and has not noticed the enormous expenditure in the Post Office Department. I was startled when I looked over the blue-book published by the Government this year to see that such an enormous expenditure has been made in that department. I think that the Post Office Department is the greatest example we have of extravagance. In the year 1910-11, the last year for which we on this side could be held responsible, the total expenditure in the Post Office Department wa's $7,954,222. The expenditures for the following years were: 1911-12,	$9,172,035;	1912-13,	$10,882,804;
1913-14, $12,822,058; 1914-15, $15,961,191. For 1916-17 the minister is asking for $17,486,727. Comparing the expenditure of 1910-11 with the proposed expenditure for 1916-17, you will find that there is an increase of $9,932,504, practically $10,000,000. That is more than double. In the four years this Government has been in power the

total expenditure has grown from $8,000,000 to $17,000,000; that is $1,000,000 more than double the expenditure of the late Administration. That is a very extravagant record for the Post Office Department, and it cannot all be attributed to the rural mail delivery or the explanations given by my hon. friend this afternoon. I quite understand that he was interrupted frequently and perhaps did not have proper opportunity to make his case, as I would have liked to see him make it. But at the same time I think it is impossible for the minister to justify an increase of $10,000,000 in the Post Office Department during the time the present Government has been in power. The hon. member for Rouville (Mr. Lemieux) asked some questions in regard to this matter in the effort to ascertain why the expenditure had grown so enormously. He asked the increase in the number of regular, salaried employees in the ten cities: Montreal, Toronto, Quebec, Winnipeg, Vancouver, Three Rivers, Sherbrooke, Halifax, St. John, and Victoria. The number of employees in those ten cities at the time the late Government went out of power was 2,208. At the present time the number is 3,630, an increase of 1,422 salaried employees appointed during the present administration. That is an enormous increase, and I do not think it can be justified by the increase in the business. In looking at the revenue I find that while the expenditure during those four years has increased by $10,000,000 there is no corresponding increase in the revenue. The revenue for the last year for which we have the figures is practically $4,000,000 more than it was when the present Government came into power. That is an increase of $4,000,000 in four years, and I have no doubt it is largely accounted for by appointments to office that should not have been made, and by extravagance in the letting of contracts. I do not know how else to account for it, and' I would like to see some better account given of that enormous increase of expenditure than has been given by my hon. friend this afternoon. I think it remains yet to be explained, and I trust that when we meet again to deal with the Estimates of the Post Office Department, the minister will, in some concise way, tell us what justification there is for an increase of $10,000,000 during the short time this Government has been in power.*
